    "id": "10.14293/pr2199.004798.v1",
    "title": "Recombinant DNA Topoisomerases for Biomedical Research and Pharmaceutical Discovery: Expression Platforms, Functional Assays, and Translational Applications",
    "abstract": "DNA topoisomerases regulate DNA topology during replication, transcription, recombination, and chromosome segregation. Several members of this enzyme family are established targets of anticancer and antibacterial drugs. Recombinant production of topoisomerases supports biochemical screening, structural biology, and mechanistic pharmacology. This narrative review examines the classification and catalytic mechanisms of DNA topoisomerases, recombinant expression platforms in bacteria, yeast, and human cells, purification and quality-control considerations, functional assays, and translational applications in drug discovery. Particular attention is given to DNA relaxation, decatenation, ATPase activity, and cleavage-complex assays, as well as counter-screening strategies to distinguish genuine enzyme inhibition from nonspecific effects. The review compares the strengths and limitations of different expression systems and emphasizes that protein yield alone is insufficient to establish platform suitability; enzymatic activity, isoform identity, and appropriate assay controls must also be demonstrated. It further discusses topoisomerase poisons and catalytic inhibitors, including approaches to TOP2A/TOP2B isoform selectivity. Topobexin is discussed as a preclinical TOP2B-selective catalytic inhibitor with potential relevance to anthracycline-associated cardiotoxicity. Overall, recombinant topoisomerase systems provide valuable tools for biomedical research and pharmaceutical discovery, although biochemical findings require further cellular and preclinical validation before clinical translation.",
