    "id": "10.21203/rs.3.rs-11195427/v1",
    "title": "Genetic Algorithm-Based Three-Dimensional UAV Swarm Trajectory Design for Direction-of-Arrival Estimation",
    "abstract": "Abstract  Unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) swarms equipped with distributed antenna arrays provide a flexible approach to direction-of-arrival (DOA) estimation over large three-dimensional (3D) apertures. However, large inter-UAV separations, required by practical flight and collision-avoidance constraints, can introduce phase ambiguities and complicate coherent array processing. This study presents a simulation-based framework for the design and evaluation of a 3D UAV antenna array for two-dimensional DOA estimation. A constraint-aware genetic algorithm (GA) is used to optimize the spatial configuration and trajectories of the UAV-mounted antenna elements by minimizing a Cramér–Rao lower bound (CRLB)-based objective while satisfying inter-element separation and operational constraints. The optimized configuration is subsequently evaluated using interferometric phase-ambiguity resolution and MUSIC-based DOA estimation. Full-wave electromagnetic simulations in CST Studio Suite are used to assess the effects of UAV structural scattering, shadowing, mutual coupling, and radiation-pattern distortion. Monte Carlo simulations further quantify DOA estimation performance under different signal-to-noise ratios and phase and gain uncertainties. The results show that the optimized configuration provides lower estimation errors than the investigated reference configurations within the simulated conditions. The study demonstrates the importance of jointly considering array geometry, phase ambiguity, signal-processing performance, and electromagnetic effects when evaluating distributed UAV antenna arrays.",
