    "id": "10.21203/rs.3.rs-11485353/v1",
    "title": "The Generalist Strikes Back:\\\\A General LLM Agent Outperforms Specialist Bioinformatics Agents on Free-Form, End-to-End Benchmark Analysis",
    "abstract": "Abstract  Specialist agents such as Biomni, STELLA and ToolsGenie wrap large language models in curated tools and multi-agent scaffolds for bioinformatics. We asked whether a current frontier model in an off-the-shelf coding harness still needs them. Claude Opus 5.5 (high effort) in Claude Code solved all 244 tasks of PromptBio-Bench, whose free-form output files are scored against expert references. It was run with and without 667 bioinformatics Agent Skills (bioSkills, ClawBio). Under the original evaluation framework with GPT-5.6 judges, Claude with skills completed every task and reached 0.869 accuracy, against 0.758 for Biomni and ToolsGenie and 0.717 for STELLA (paired McNemar p ≤ 2 × 10−4). Its failures exposed eleven evaluator defects and several incorrect reference answers. With these defects fixed, Claude reached 0.918 to 0.926 with skills and 0.873 to 0.902 without, across GPT and Claude judges that agreed on 95 to 98% of tasks. Skills added only 2.5 to 4.5 points, yet transcripts showed the agent never invoked one; the gap traces to run-to-run variation in a few judgement calls. Of the 18 tasks still failed, 11 trace to defective references or unstated settings, and most of the rest are defensible alternatives to the reference. A general-purpose frontier model now matches or exceeds purpose-built bioinformatics agents on realistic analyses; benchmark reliability, not agent capability, has become the main limit on measuring progress.",
