    "id": "10.64898/2026.11.16.766249",
    "title": "Efficient Method for Estimating the Frequency of Plasmidless Cell Generation per Cell Division",
    "abstract": "Plasmids are self-replicating pieces of DNA that play a major role in the evolution and spread of adaptive genes in bacteria. Plasmids tend to form stable associations with their hosts, including plasmids such as small, multicopy plasmids that typically lack specialized partition and horizontal transfer systems. Using a ColE1 plasmid model, we present a method that quantifies the frequency of plasmid loss over a short, defined time window. This method is based on the transient survival of plasmidless cells in the presence of selecting antibiotic and it has two main advantages over plasmid visualization approaches: (1) it does not require modifications in plasmid sequence, preventing artifactual results linked to sequence alteration, and (2) it can unambiguously resolve plasmidless cells relative to cells with a low plasmid copy number. Thus, our approach is ideal for the study of the stability of naturally-occurring small, multicopy plasmids or for the identification of modulators of stability. Using our method, we describe a mutant whose long-term stability is exclusively dependent on increased accurate segregation. Finally, we report that moderate sequence manipulations (outside ori sequence) do not have a major impact on early plasmid loss, which is relevant for the design of plasmid tagging approaches.",
